{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790423092,"nonce":"nNJbrqypCg4c/JJNpJB3hxsQnHM=","title":"Top-level code: install the early top tier without waiting for its baseline","message":"10e8418 queues a module body's top tier beside its baseline; a top-tier result that finishes first is held until the baseline installs, because FuncBody::Native needs a baseline executable and deopts resume the baseline. For run-once code the baseline is only a fallback. Let the top tier install over an interpreted function (deopts resume the interpreter), and consider not compiling the baseline at all for such bodies. On method_call this is the remaining compile latency (baseline 3-6 ms, optimised ~8 ms on the NUC). Part of 990178a / ccc061d.","files":null}]}